# Coding standards

- Follow the conventions already present in the touched module (naming, layout, formatter). If a linter/formatter config exists, it is the authority.
- Search for existing helpers before writing new ones. No copy-paste duplication.
- Handle errors explicitly: no empty `catch`/`except`, no swallowed failures. Fail with actionable messages.
- Log at boundaries (I/O, external calls, failures) with a consistent logger. Never log secrets, tokens, credentials or personal data.
- Configuration comes from environment variables or config files; never hard-code secrets. Document new variables in `.env.example` (no real values).
- Validate all external input at the boundary. Prefer parameterized queries and safe APIs over string building.
- Keep functions small and cohesive. Add comments only for non-obvious intent.
- New behavior requires tests in `tests/` (see `testing.mdc`). Public API changes require docs (see `documentation.mdc`).
- Add a dependency only if the standard library or existing dependencies cannot reasonably do the job; justify it in the plan.
